# Fork additions. Everything above is upstream's own ignore list, kept intact
# and unedited. No pattern above it swallows a Zig path: `build/*` names a
# directory that does not exist here and does not match `build.zig`, and
# upstream lists neither dot-directories nor subdirectories wholesale.
#
# `zig build` creates these directories at the repository root. They are build
# products, so they stay out of commits, and neither appears in the `.paths`
# allowlist in build.zig.zon, so an ordinary user build cannot change the
# package hash.
.zig-cache/
zig-cache/
zig-out/

# `consumer/` is a package in its own right: `zig build run` inside it creates
# its own output tree. It is listed here as well as in consumer/.gitignore so
# that a run from the repository root cannot be committed by accident. An
# operator who redirects the cache with --cache-dir owns that path instead.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts. `nix build` writes `result` into the
# directory that asked for it, and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild;
# `devenv` writes its resolved inputs and its state beside the project. None of
# them is named in build.zig.zon `.paths`, so an ignored path can never become
# part of the package. `flake.lock` and `devenv.lock` are deliberately *not*
# here: they are real inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would
# be the wrong way to keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
